 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "A_hook",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Ethan Couch, 16, driving drunk, killed four people in a crash in Burleson, Texas, on 15 June 2013; in December 2013 he was sentenced (juvenile court) to ten years' probation with no jail or prison time. Narration: 'He killed four people. He got no prison.' Headline: '4 DEAD. NO PRISON.'",
   "sources": [
    "ap_sent",
    "fox13",
    "cbs_fam",
    "slate",
    "wiki",
    "ap_rehab"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_affluenza",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The defense presented a psychologist who said Couch was a product of 'affluenza' (a privileged, permissive upbringing). Narration: 'His defense? He grew up too rich.' (the hot-take framing of 'affluenza'; the next scene states the testimony). The judge did not say affluenza was the basis of her decision (recorded, see A_judge).",
   "sources": [
    "wiki",
    "slate",
    "inquirer",
    "chn_miller",
    "ap_sent"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_crash",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "CARD: the crash was on 15 June 2013 on Burleson-Retta Road, Burleson, Texas; he was driving his father's pickup (reported as a 2012 Ford F-350) with passengers, at about 70 mph in a 40 mph zone (Wikipedia). The crash itself is not shown.",
   "sources": [
    "wiki",
    "kvue_lives",
    "ap_sent"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_bac",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Prosecutors: a test about three hours after the crash showed a blood alcohol content of 0.24, three times the legal limit; he was 16. Narration: 'He was 16, three times the legal limit.'",
   "sources": [
    "ap_sent",
    "fox13",
    "wiki"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_victims",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "MEMORIAL CARD: the four killed were Breanna Mitchell, 24, whose SUV had stalled, and three people who had stopped to help her: Hollie Boyles, 52, her daughter Shelby Boyles, 21, and youth pastor Brian Jennings, 41. Several others were injured (reported as nine).",
   "sources": [
    "wiki",
    "kvue_lives",
    "fox4_vict",
    "fox5_vict"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_expert",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Defense psychologist G. Dick Miller testified that Couch was a product of 'affluenza': affluent parents who gave him freedoms no young person should have and never set boundaries or consequences. Narration: 'A defense expert called it affluenza.'; label sub 'Rich parents, no boundaries, he said'.",
   "sources": [
    "wiki",
    "inquirer",
    "chn_miller",
    "slate"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_notdiag",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "LABEL: 'affluenza' is not a recognised medical diagnosis (American Psychiatric Association); Miller later said he regretted using the term.",
   "sources": [
    "globe",
    "wiki",
    "chn_miller"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_ask",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Prosecutors had asked for the maximum, 20 years. Narration: 'Prosecutors had asked for 20 years.'",
   "sources": [
    "fox13",
    "ap_sent",
    "wiki"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_sentence",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "In December 2013 (reported 10-11 December) Judge Jean Boyd sentenced him to ten years' probation and ordered residential treatment; no jail time. Narration: 'But he got ten years' probation. No jail.'",
   "sources": [
    "ap_sent",
    "fox13",
    "cbs_fam",
    "slate",
    "wiki",
    "ap_rehab"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_judge",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "BOTH SIDES (judge): the judge chose probation with residential treatment (rehab) over the prison term prosecutors sought; she said 'affluenza' was not the basis of her decision; a juvenile-justice advocate said that, if the aim is rehabilitation, it was the right decision. Narration: 'The judge chose rehab over prison.'",
   "sources": [
    "ap_rehab",
    "fox13",
    "wiki",
    "cbs_fam"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_father",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "BOTH SIDES (families): Eric Boyles, who lost his wife Hollie and daughter Shelby, said the result would have been different had Couch not had money for the defense, the experts and the treatment, and that money and privilege cannot buy justice. Narration: 'A victim's father blamed the money.' (paraphrase; no quote is put on screen)",
   "sources": [
    "cbs_fam",
    "fox13",
    "ap_sent"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_video",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "LABEL: in December 2015 a short video circulating on Twitter appeared to show him at a party where people played beer pong (alcohol was banned under his probation); he then missed a probation appointment (10 December 2015).",
   "sources": [
    "cbs_video",
    "fox4_video",
    "root_video",
    "fox_mx"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_fled",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He and his mother, Tonya Couch, fled to Mexico; they were detained in Puerto Vallarta on 28 December 2015. Narration: 'Then he fled to Mexico.'; label sub 'He fled with his mother'.",
   "sources": [
    "fox_mx",
    "fortune",
    "globe",
    "star"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_pizza",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "FUN FACT CARD: according to the Jalisco state prosecutors' report, a U.S. Marshals Service agent tipped Mexican authorities to the location of a phone that had been used to order Domino's pizza in Puerto Vallarta; that led to the pair. Narration: 'A pizza order helped give them away.'",
   "sources": [
    "globe",
    "star",
    "kvue_pizza",
    "newsone"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_lesson",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "LESSON CARD (practical, for the viewer; NHTSA): before you have even one drink, designate a sober driver; your options to get home safely are a sober driver, a taxi or a rideshare; if a friend is about to drive drunk, take their keys and find them a safe way home. Rows: 'PICK A SOBER DRIVER FIRST', 'OR A TAXI OR RIDESHARE', \"TAKE A DRUNK MATE'S KEYS\".",
   "sources": [
    "nhtsa_buzz",
    "nhtsa_hol",
    "nhtsa_july4",
    "nhtsa_tips",
    "nhtsa_ctw",
    "vadmv"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_720",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "STATISTIC (counter): after his return, with the case in adult court, Judge Wayne Salvant ordered four consecutive 180-day jail terms, one for each person killed: 720 days, as a condition of probation (April 2016). Narration: 'Back in Texas, he got 720 days.'",
   "sources": [
    "nbc_720",
    "ktla_720",
    "root_720",
    "npr_rel"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_release",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "CARD: he was released from the Tarrant County jail on 2 April 2018 after serving the 720 days, to finish his probation under GPS and alcohol monitoring and a curfew.",
   "sources": [
    "npr_rel",
    "kera_rel",
    "cbs_rel",
    "ky3_rel"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A_180",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The 720 days were four consecutive 180-day terms, one for each of the four people killed. Narration (last line): 'That is 180 days for each life taken.'",
   "sources": [
    "nbc_720",
    "root_720",
    "ktla_720"
   ]
  }
 ],
